      AppLesson(
        title: 'Know Your Reader Before You Write a Word',
        body:
            "The single biggest lever in copywriting isn't clever wordplay — it's understanding exactly who you're writing for. Vague targeting ('young Nigerians who like fashion') produces vague, forgettable copy. Specific targeting ('a 24-year-old university graduate in Lagos, currently job-hunting, who wants to look sharp for interviews but has a tight budget') produces copy that speaks directly and specifically to a real person's actual situation, because you can reference their exact circumstances, fears, and desires.\n\nBefore writing any piece of copy, answer these concretely: what does this specific reader want right now? What's stopping them from getting it (money, time, doubt, lack of information)? What have they probably already tried that didn't work? What objection will they raise in their head the moment they see your offer? Copy that pre-answers a reader's unspoken objections converts far better than copy that ignores them and hopes the reader won't think of them.\n\nA practical technique many working copywriters use is writing a one-paragraph profile of a single, specific imagined reader — giving them a name, an age, a specific frustration, and a specific desire — and then writing the entire piece as a message to that one person, rather than to a vague, faceless 'audience.' Writing to one specific person, oddly, tends to resonate with far more real readers than writing to an imagined crowd.",
