        body:
            "A feature is a fact about a product — 'this blender has a 1000-watt motor.' A benefit is what that fact actually means for the reader's life — 'crush frozen ingredients into smooth pap in seconds, even before your morning coffee kicks in.' Beginners write features because features are easy to list from a spec sheet; professionals translate every feature into a benefit, because readers don't buy specifications, they buy outcomes and feelings.\n\nA simple, reliable technique for this translation is the 'so what?' test: state a feature, then ask 'so what?' and answer honestly, then ask 'so what?' again on that answer, repeating until you reach something the reader genuinely, emotionally cares about. '1000-watt motor' → so what? → 'it crushes ice and frozen fruit easily' → so what? → 'you can make a proper smoothie in the morning without waiting or struggling' → so what? → 'you actually stick to your health goals because it's not a hassle.' That final answer, not the wattage, is what should anchor your headline.\n\nNot every feature needs the full benefit treatment in every piece of copy — sometimes a feature is stated plainly as supporting proof once the core benefit has already been established emotionally. But the core persuasive weight of almost any piece of copy should sit on benefits, with features serving as credible backup evidence for why the reader can trust that benefit is real and achievable.",
