      AppLesson(
        title: 'The AIDA Formula: A Reliable Starting Structure',
        body:
            "AIDA — Attention, Interest, Desire, Action — is one of the oldest and most reliable structural formulas in copywriting, and while not every piece of copy needs to follow it rigidly, understanding it gives you a dependable starting skeleton, especially useful when you're staring at a blank page and unsure where to begin.\n\nAttention is your opening hook — a headline or first line that stops the reader's scroll or catches their eye before anything else happens; without attention, nothing else in the copy matters because the reader never engages with it at all. Interest builds on that hook by giving the reader a specific reason to keep reading — usually by speaking directly to their situation, problem, or curiosity in a way that feels personally relevant rather than generic.\n\nDesire is where you build emotional and logical want for the specific solution you're offering, often using benefits, social proof, or vivid description of the outcome. Action is the final, explicit instruction telling the reader exactly what to do next — click here, call now, reply 'YES' — and it should never be vague or left implied, because readers who are persuaded but not given a clear next step often simply do nothing at all.",
