        body:
            "On average, roughly 80% of readers will only ever read your headline and never make it to the body copy at all — meaning the headline alone is responsible for the vast majority of whether a piece of copy succeeds or fails, regardless of how strong the rest of the writing is. A weak headline means a strong body of copy is essentially wasted, because almost nobody reads far enough to encounter it.\n\nStrong headlines typically do one of a few reliable things: promise a clear, specific benefit ('Cut your data spend in half this month'), provoke curiosity without being misleading ('The one mistake killing your online sales'), or address a specific, urgent problem directly ('Struggling to get replies on WhatsApp Business? Here's why'). Vague, clever-for-its-own-sake headlines that don't clearly signal what's in it for the reader tend to underperform specific, benefit-driven ones significantly.\n\nWrite far more headline options than you think you need — professional copywriters routinely write 15-25 headline variations for a single piece of copy before selecting or testing the strongest ones, because the first few ideas that come to mind are usually the most generic, obvious ones everyone else would also think of first. The genuinely strong headline is often the eighth, twelfth, or twentieth one you write, after you've exhausted the easy, predictable options.",
