      AppLesson(
        title: 'Urgency and Scarcity — Used Honestly',
        body:
            "Urgency (a time-limited reason to act now) and scarcity (a limited-quantity reason to act now) are genuinely effective at overcoming procrastination, which is one of the biggest silent killers of conversion — a reader who is persuaded but not given a reason to act immediately will often simply close the tab intending to 'come back later,' and never actually return.\n\nThe critical rule is that urgency and scarcity must be real. A 'sale ends tonight!' that reappears identically every single day trains readers to recognize the claim as false, which doesn't just fail to create urgency — it actively damages trust in every future claim you make, including genuinely true ones. If a discount really does end at a specific time, or a batch really is limited to a specific quantity, state that honestly; don't invent false countdown timers or fake 'only 2 left in stock' claims that don't reflect reality.\n\nBeyond fabricated deadlines, urgency can be created honestly through genuinely limited circumstances — a seasonal product that's only relevant for a specific window, a cohort-based course that only opens enrollment periodically, a genuinely limited batch of handmade goods. Building your offer structure around real constraints, rather than manufacturing fake ones, gives you honest urgency that holds up to scrutiny and protects long-term customer trust.",
