        body:
            "On any platform where people are scrolling — social media, search results, an inbox full of unread emails — you are competing for attention against dozens of other pieces of content within a window of roughly two to three seconds. In that window, a reader makes an almost instant, largely unconscious decision: keep looking at this, or move on. Your headline or opening hook is entirely responsible for winning that decision.\n\nThis means a hook's job is narrower and more specific than most beginners assume — it doesn't need to explain your whole offer, tell your whole story, or list every benefit. Its only job is to earn the next few seconds of attention, creating enough curiosity, relevance, or emotional pull that the reader chooses to keep going instead of scrolling past. Everything else in the piece has its own job to do later; the hook's job is strictly to survive that first three-second filter.\n\nUnderstanding this narrow job changes how you should evaluate a hook during revision — don't ask 'does this cover everything important?' Ask instead: 'if a stressed, distracted person glanced at only this line for one second, would it make them pause?' If the honest answer is no, the hook needs to be sharper and more specific, not longer or more comprehensive.",
