        body:
            "A curiosity gap is the space between what a reader currently knows and what they want to know, and a well-built hook deliberately opens that gap without fully closing it — giving the reader just enough information to know a gap exists, but withholding the specific answer, which creates a genuine, almost itchy pull to keep reading and close that gap themselves.\n\n'The one habit that changed how I save money' opens a curiosity gap (what habit?) without answering it. 'I started saving 20% more every month by writing my expenses in a notebook every night' closes the gap immediately in the headline itself, leaving nothing left to discover — which often reduces the incentive to actually click through or keep reading, because the reader already has the core payoff.\n\nCurious gaps must be honestly closeable — the eventual answer needs to actually satisfy the specific curiosity you opened, or readers feel tricked once they discover the payoff doesn't match the tease, which is sometimes called 'clickbait' when done badly and dishonestly. The skill isn't withholding information forever; it's withholding it just long enough, and delivering a genuinely satisfying answer once the reader has invested the attention to find it.",
