            "Specific numbers and details make a hook instantly more credible and concrete than vague generalities. '5 ways to grow your business' feels more trustworthy and scannable than 'how to grow your business,' because the number signals structure, effort, and a defined scope the reader can quickly evaluate before committing to read further. Oddly specific numbers ('I made ₦47,000 in my first week') often outperform round ones ('I made ₦50,000'), because the precision reads as more authentic and less like a made-up marketing claim.\n\nVague hooks fail because they force the reader to do interpretive work to figure out if the content is even relevant to them — 'tips for better writing' could mean almost anything, while 'the exact 3-step editing process I use before publishing any article' tells the reader precisely what they're getting and lets them make an instant, confident decision about whether to keep reading.\n\nWhenever you draft a hook, actively hunt for any vague word — 'better,' 'great,' 'amazing,' 'effective' — and challenge yourself to replace it with something concrete and specific instead. This single habit, applied consistently, tends to improve hook quality more reliably than almost any other single technique, because specificity does most of the persuasive work that vague enthusiasm only pretends to do.",
