        body:
            "There's a meaningful, important difference between a strong curiosity-driven hook and dishonest clickbait, even though both techniques can look superficially similar on the surface. A strong hook creates genuine curiosity that the following content then honestly satisfies. Clickbait creates a promise or implication the content doesn't actually deliver on, leaving the reader feeling misled or cheated once they've invested their attention.\n\nClickbait might produce short-term clicks, but it reliably destroys long-term trust — readers who feel tricked once become measurably less likely to click on anything from that same source again, and in commercial contexts, this directly damages a brand's reputation and future conversion rates, making clickbait a poor long-term strategy even judged purely on business results, not just on ethical grounds.\n\nA reliable self-check before publishing any hook: read the hook, then read the content that follows it, and ask honestly whether a genuinely reasonable person would feel satisfied that the hook's implicit promise was actually delivered. If the content doesn't fully deliver what the hook implied, the fix is either strengthening the content to genuinely earn that hook, or softening the hook to more honestly reflect what the content actually offers — never simply hoping the reader won't notice the gap.",
