            "Search intent is the actual underlying goal behind a specific search query, and writing content that matches a keyword's exact words but not its underlying intent is one of the most common reasons SEO content fails to rank or convert, even when the target keyword appears prominently and naturally throughout the piece. Search intent is generally categorized into a few types: informational (wants to learn something), navigational (wants to find a specific website or page), commercial investigation (comparing options before a decision), and transactional (ready to buy or take action right now).\n\nA search for 'what is SEO copywriting' carries clear informational intent — the searcher wants an explanation, and content trying to sell a course aggressively right at the top of that page, before answering the actual question, will frustrate that intent and likely rank and convert poorly despite technically including the right keyword. A search for 'buy SEO copywriting course' carries clear transactional intent, where a similar hard-sell approach would actually be appropriate and expected.\n\nBefore writing SEO content for any target keyword, actually search that exact phrase yourself and study what's currently ranking on the first page — the type of content already ranking well (guides, product pages, comparison lists, videos) is strong direct evidence of what search intent Google's algorithm has already determined that specific keyword represents, and matching your content's format and approach to that established intent significantly improves your odds of ranking alongside it.",
