      AppLesson(
        title: 'On-Page SEO: Where Keywords Actually Belong',
        body:
            "On-page SEO refers to specific, deliberate placements of your target keyword and related terms within a piece of content that help search engines understand what the page is about, without requiring unnatural repetition throughout the body text itself. The page title (often called the title tag) is one of the most heavily weighted signals — it should include your primary target keyword naturally, ideally near the beginning, while still reading as a genuine, compelling headline a human would want to click.\n\nThe first paragraph of body content, subheadings throughout the piece, and the meta description (the short summary snippet that appears beneath your page's title in search results) are all valuable places to naturally include your target keyword and closely related terms — but 'naturally' is the operative word; if including the keyword in a specific spot requires genuinely awkward, unnatural phrasing, it's better to leave it out of that specific spot and trust the keyword's presence elsewhere on the page.\n\nImage alt text (a short written description attached to images, primarily for accessibility purposes for visually impaired readers using screen readers) is also read and used as a ranking signal by search engines, and represents another natural, legitimate place to reference relevant terms — describing what the image actually shows accurately, which often naturally overlaps with relevant keywords when the image is genuinely topically relevant to the surrounding content.",
