      AppLesson(
        title: 'Writing for Featured Snippets and Voice Search',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "A featured snippet is the highlighted answer box that sometimes appears at the very top of Google's search results, directly answering a query without the searcher even needing to click through to a website — earning this placement, sometimes informally called 'position zero,' can drive substantial traffic even from a lower overall ranking position, because it's the very first thing many searchers see and read.\n\nContent structured to directly and concisely answer a specific question — often within the first sentence or two immediately following a clear subheading phrased as that exact question — has a meaningfully better chance of being selected as a featured snippet than content where the actual answer is buried deep within a long, meandering paragraph. Numbered lists and clearly formatted step-by-step instructions are also frequently pulled into snippet format for how-to style queries specifically.\n\nVoice search (queries spoken aloud to Siri, Google Assistant, or similar) tends to closely mirror featured snippet content, since voice assistants very often read the featured snippet answer aloud as their spoken response. Voice queries also tend to be phrased more conversationally and as full questions compared to typed searches, so including natural, conversational question phrasing within your subheadings can help capture this specific, and increasingly significant, search behavior pattern.",
