      AppLesson(
        title: 'Content Length and Depth: Longer Isn\'t Always Better',
        body:
            "There's a persistent myth in SEO circles that longer content automatically ranks better, but the real relationship is more accurately: content that thoroughly and genuinely answers a searcher's question tends to rank well, and thorough answers to complex questions often happen to require more length — length is a common side effect of genuine thoroughness, not a ranking factor in and of itself that you should chase directly.\n\nA simple, narrow question genuinely deserves a short, direct answer, and artificially padding that answer with unnecessary filler content purely to hit an arbitrary word count target tends to hurt user experience and, consequently, hurt rankings as well, since search algorithms increasingly account for genuine engagement signals like how long readers actually stay on a page and whether they quickly bounce back to the search results dissatisfied.\n\nBefore writing, honestly assess how much genuine depth your specific target question actually requires and deserves, and match your content length to that honest assessment — a genuinely comprehensive guide on a complex topic might reasonably run several thousand words, while a specific factual question ('what does gsm mean in paper') deserves a concise, direct, complete answer, and stretching it unnecessarily to appear more substantial serves neither the reader nor, ultimately, your search ranking.",
