        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Internal links are links from one page on your own website to another page on that same website, and they serve two important functions: helping readers naturally discover more of your relevant content (improving overall engagement and time spent on your site), and helping search engines understand the relationships and relative importance between different pages on your site, since more heavily internally-linked pages are generally interpreted as more significant.\n\nWhen writing new content, actively look for genuine, relevant opportunities to link to your own other existing, related content — but only where it's genuinely useful and relevant to the reader in that specific context, not as forced, unnatural insertions purely for SEO benefit. A genuine, contextually relevant internal link ('for a full breakdown of keyword research tools, see our separate guide here') serves both the reader's actual interest and the technical SEO benefit simultaneously.\n\nClear content structure — logically organized subheadings that break a long piece into genuinely distinct, scannable sections — also benefits both readers and search engines together. Readers scanning quickly can find the specific section relevant to their exact question, and search engines use this same subheading structure to better understand the specific subtopics your overall content actually covers in meaningful depth.",
