      AppLesson(
        title: 'Writing SEO Copy That Doesn\'t Sound Like SEO Copy',
        body:
            "The clearest sign of poor SEO copywriting is content that reads as though it was written for a search algorithm first and a human reader second — unnaturally repeated keyword phrases, awkward sentence construction bent to fit a specific keyword string, and a generally robotic, joyless tone that no genuine human would choose to write in if search rankings weren't a consideration at all.\n\nThe professional standard is writing genuinely excellent, natural content first, with SEO best practices applied as a secondary, integrated layer rather than the primary driving force behind sentence-level word choices. If a specific keyword phrase doesn't fit naturally into a sentence without contorting the grammar or rhythm, either find a more natural synonym or variant phrasing that still signals the same topic to search engines, or simply trust that the keyword's presence elsewhere on the page is sufficient without forcing it awkwardly into every single paragraph.\n\nA genuinely useful test: read your finished SEO content aloud to someone with zero knowledge of your specific target keyword, and ask if anything sounds unnatural, repetitive, or stilted. If they can identify a keyword-stuffed sentence purely from how strange it sounds when spoken, that sentence needs revision — natural, human-quality writing that happens to be genuinely relevant to a topic is, in the current search landscape, also the most effective SEO writing available.",
