        body:
            "A subject line's only job, much like a headline's, is to earn the open — it doesn't need to summarize the entire email or make the full sale, it just needs to be compelling enough that the recipient chooses to click into a crowded inbox competing against dozens of other unread messages. Personalization (including the recipient's first name, or referencing their specific past behavior or purchase) can measurably improve open rates, because it signals immediate relevance rather than a generic mass broadcast.\n\nCuriosity and specificity, covered in depth in the headlines course, apply directly to subject lines as well — 'Your order update' is vague and easily ignored; 'Your order ships tomorrow — here's what to expect' is specific and creates a small, satisfying reason to open. Urgency, when genuinely honest, also works well in subject lines specifically, since email inboxes create a natural sense of a growing, competing backlog that time-sensitive language can leverage effectively.\n\nAvoid subject lines that trigger spam filters or feel manipulative — excessive capitalization, multiple exclamation points, and certain heavily-flagged words ('free,' 'guarantee,' 'act now' used in isolation without genuine context) can hurt deliverability technically, in addition to feeling manipulative to a genuinely attentive human reader. Test subject line variations where your email platform allows it, since — exactly as with ad headlines — real audience data consistently beats internal guesswork about what specifically will earn the open.",
