        body:
            "Most people scan emails rather than reading them word for word, particularly on mobile devices where screen space is limited and attention is often genuinely divided between several things happening at once — writing dense, unbroken paragraphs of email copy, however well-crafted the individual sentences might be, tends to be skipped or abandoned by readers who simply don't have the patience to parse a wall of text in an inbox context.\n\nShort paragraphs (often just one to three sentences), generous white space, and a single clear focus per email all improve scannability and, consequently, actual engagement and follow-through. An email trying to communicate five different messages and drive five different actions simultaneously tends to accomplish none of them particularly well — one email, one primary goal, one primary call to action is a more reliable structure than trying to pack in everything at once.\n\nBullet points and short, punchy sentences work especially well in email specifically, more so than in a longer-form article or landing page, precisely because of the scanning behavior most recipients bring to their inbox — reserve longer, flowing paragraphs for moments genuinely worth slowing a reader down for, like a personal story or an emotionally significant point, and use tighter, more compressed formatting for everything else surrounding it.",
