      AppLesson(
        title: 'Segmentation: Not Every Subscriber Wants the Same Email',
        body:
            "Segmentation means dividing your email list into distinct groups based on genuine differences — past purchase behavior, stated interests, how they originally joined your list, how engaged they've been with previous emails — and tailoring your messaging specifically to each segment, rather than sending every single subscriber the exact same generic broadcast regardless of their actual relevance to that specific message.\n\nA subscriber who has already purchased a specific product doesn't need to keep receiving generic introductory emails about that same product's basic benefits — they're better served by content about advanced usage, complementary products, or loyalty offers, while a brand-new subscriber who hasn't purchased anything yet is better served by exactly the foundational, trust-building content that an existing customer has already moved past.\n\nEven basic segmentation — as simple as separating 'has purchased before' from 'has not purchased yet,' or 'opened the last three emails' from 'hasn't opened anything in two months' — meaningfully improves relevance and, consequently, engagement and conversion rates, compared to a single undifferentiated blast to an entire list regardless of where each individual subscriber actually sits in their relationship with your brand.",
