        body:
            "Every email list naturally accumulates subscribers who gradually stop opening or engaging with emails over time — rather than continuing to email disengaged subscribers indefinitely with no acknowledgment, a dedicated re-engagement or 'win-back' email sequence specifically targets these inactive subscribers, either reviving their interest or identifying that they should genuinely be removed from the active list.\n\nA win-back email often works well by directly, honestly acknowledging the lapse in engagement ('we've missed you — it's been a while') paired with either a genuinely valuable incentive to return (a special offer specifically for lapsed subscribers) or a simple, low-pressure question asking what content they'd actually find valuable going forward, treating the disengagement as useful feedback rather than simply a problem to overcome through pressure.\n\nMaintaining list health by periodically removing genuinely unengaged subscribers who don't respond even to a dedicated win-back attempt, rather than keeping an inflated but largely unresponsive list indefinitely, actually improves overall email deliverability and engagement metrics over time — email providers increasingly weigh genuine engagement rates when deciding whether future emails land in a recipient's main inbox or get filtered into spam, making list health a meaningfully practical, not just aesthetic, business consideration.",
