      AppLesson(
        title: 'Measuring What Actually Matters in Email',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Open rate (the percentage of recipients who opened an email) and click-through rate (the percentage who clicked a link within it) are the two most commonly tracked email metrics, and understanding what each one actually tells you — and doesn't tell you — is essential for genuinely improving your email copywriting over time, rather than optimizing for the wrong signal.\n\nA low open rate primarily points to a subject line problem (or a genuine deliverability/spam filtering issue), since the subject line is the only thing a recipient sees before deciding whether to open at all — improving subject line craft, following the earlier lesson's checklist, is the most direct lever for improving this specific metric. A low click-through rate, on an email that was actually opened at a healthy rate, points instead to a body copy or CTA problem — the subject line succeeded at earning attention, but the content or the call to action inside failed to convert that attention into action.\n\nUltimately, conversion rate (the percentage of recipients who completed the actual desired end goal — a purchase, a signup, a booking) is the metric that matters most commercially, since opens and clicks are useful diagnostic signals along the way but aren't themselves the actual business outcome you're working toward. Track all three together, and use the specific pattern across them to diagnose exactly where in your funnel — subject line, body copy, or offer itself — genuinely needs the most revision attention.",
