      AppLesson(
        title: 'Repurposing One Idea Across Multiple Posts',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "A single strong underlying idea, insight, or piece of content rarely needs to be used only once — professional social media copywriters routinely repurpose one core idea into several different formats and angles across multiple posts and even multiple platforms, extracting significantly more total value from the original thinking or research effort that went into developing that one idea in the first place.\n\nA single useful insight might become a short, punchy Twitter/X post, a more detailed, story-driven Instagram caption paired with a relevant image, a professionally-framed LinkedIn post connecting it to broader industry insight, and a numbered list format post breaking the same idea down into a scannable checklist — each version reformatted and re-voiced appropriately for its specific platform, following the platform-specific voice principles from earlier in this course, while still communicating the same fundamentally strong core idea.\n\nBuilding a simple habit of noting promising ideas as they occur to you throughout the week — a useful realization, a customer question worth publicly answering, a specific mistake and lesson — and only later deciding which platform and format best suits each one, tends to produce a more sustainable and genuinely higher-quality content pipeline than trying to invent an entirely fresh, unrelated idea from nothing for every single individual post across every platform.",
      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Mini Project: One Idea, Three Platform-Native Posts',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "For your final project, choose a single core idea, insight, or story relevant to a fictional or real business, and write three distinct, platform-native versions of it — one for Instagram, one for Twitter/X, and one for LinkedIn — applying the platform-specific voice principles from this course to each.\n\nFor each version, write a genuinely strong first line specifically, since this functions as your effective hook or headline for that piece, and ensure each version is meaningfully adapted in tone, length, and structure to its specific platform, rather than being the identical text lightly reformatted across all three. Your Instagram version should include a warmer, more personal or story-driven angle; your Twitter/X version should be sharp and concise; your LinkedIn version should connect the idea to a broader professional insight or lesson.\n\nFor at least one of your three versions, include a genuine, specific engagement invitation — a question your audience could easily and specifically answer in a comment — rather than a vague, generic invitation to respond. Review all three finished versions together and honestly assess: does each one feel genuinely native to its specific platform, or does at least one still read like it was simply copied and lightly adjusted from another?",
