        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Brand voice is a business's consistent underlying personality — the way it fundamentally sounds and communicates across every single piece of writing it ever produces, regardless of context. Tone, by contrast, is how that consistent voice flexes and adjusts for a specific situation or emotional context — the same underlying voice might sound genuinely upbeat and playful in a product launch announcement, while sounding notably more measured and empathetic in a customer service response addressing a genuine complaint.\n\nA useful analogy: voice is like a person's fundamental personality, which stays essentially consistent across their entire life — someone who is naturally warm and direct remains fundamentally warm and direct whether they're at a party or at a funeral. Tone is like how that same fundamentally consistent person adjusts their specific delivery, energy, and word choice appropriately for each of those very different specific situations, without actually becoming a different person or abandoning their core personality.\n\nConfusing voice and tone leads to two common, opposite mistakes: a brand that changes its fundamental voice depending on context feels inconsistent and untrustworthy, as though a different person is writing every single piece of communication with no coherent underlying identity connecting them; a brand that never adjusts tone at all, applying an identical energy and register regardless of a genuinely different situation's emotional needs, feels tone-deaf and poorly attuned to its specific audience and context in that moment.",
