      ),
      AppLesson(
        title: 'Vocabulary and Word Choice as Voice Signals',
        body:
            "Specific, deliberate vocabulary choices are among the most concrete, actionable tools for expressing a distinct brand voice consistently — a brand aiming for an approachable, down-to-earth voice might consistently choose simple, everyday words over more formal or technical alternatives, while a brand aiming for expert authority might deliberately use precise, industry-specific terminology, trusting that its specific audience will recognize and value that particular precision.\n\nDocumenting specific words and phrases a brand consistently uses, alongside specific words and phrases it deliberately avoids, gives concrete, directly applicable guidance far beyond abstract personality adjectives alone — a brand that has decided to consistently say 'you' rather than more formal or distant alternatives, or that has decided to never use certain overused marketing clichés ('game-changing,' 'revolutionary,' 'best-in-class'), gives any writer immediately actionable, specific rules rather than vague inspirational guidance requiring individual interpretation.\n\nConsistent vocabulary choices around a brand's own specific offerings also matter significantly for both brand consistency and practical clarity — deciding once whether your product is consistently called a 'course,' a 'program,' or a 'class,' for example, and then applying that single consistent choice everywhere, prevents the subtle confusion and slightly unprofessional inconsistency that emerges when different pieces of content from the same brand describe the identical thing using several different, inconsistent terms.",
