      AppLesson(
        title: 'Tone Shifting for Different Contexts',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "Even with a clearly and consistently defined underlying voice, different specific contexts genuinely demand different tonal adjustments — a product launch announcement can reasonably lean into a brand's full, energetic personality, while a message addressing a service outage, a genuine customer complaint, or any other sensitive or difficult situation demands a more measured, empathetic, and notably less playful tonal register, even from a brand whose underlying voice is normally quite playful and casual.\n\nA useful practical exercise is mapping out several common communication contexts a business regularly encounters — a celebratory announcement, routine customer support, a genuine complaint or crisis, an educational or how-to piece of content — and explicitly noting how the core, consistent voice should tonally flex for each specific context, while still remaining unmistakably, recognizably the same underlying brand throughout every single one of those different situations.\n\nGetting tone wrong for a specific context — being playfully humorous during a genuine, serious customer complaint, for instance — can cause real, sometimes significant reputational damage far beyond simply being a stylistic misstep, because it can read to an already-frustrated customer as dismissive of a situation they're currently experiencing as genuinely serious and important to them personally, regardless of how minor that same situation might reasonably seem from the business's own broader, less personally invested perspective.",
