      AppLesson(
        title: 'Auditing Existing Content for Voice Inconsistency',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "For an established business with a significant body of existing content, a formal voice audit — reviewing a representative sample of existing copy across different channels and different time periods to specifically identify inconsistencies against a newly defined or refined voice standard — is a genuinely valuable, practical exercise before rolling out new voice guidelines broadly across an organization.\n\nDuring an audit, specifically flag content that violates the anti-adjectives defined in your voice guide (content that reads as arrogant when the intended voice is confident-but-humble, for instance), uses vocabulary explicitly on the 'avoid' list, or applies a tonal register mismatched to its specific actual context (overly casual language in what should genuinely be a more serious, sensitive communication, or vice versa).\n\nRather than attempting to immediately rewrite an organization's entire historical back catalog of content all at once, which is often impractical given real time and resource constraints, prioritize auditing and revising the highest-visibility, highest-traffic content first — a homepage, key landing pages, pinned social posts, and other prominent brand touchpoints most likely to genuinely shape a new visitor's very first impression — before working through lower-priority, lower-visibility content more gradually over time as resources genuinely allow.",
