      AppLesson(
        title: 'Evolving Brand Voice Over Time Without Losing Consistency',
        hasImage: true,
        body:
            "A brand's voice isn't necessarily meant to remain completely static and unchanging forever — as a business genuinely grows, matures, or deliberately repositions itself in its market over time, its voice may reasonably need to evolve as well, but this kind of evolution should be a deliberate, considered, well-documented decision, rather than an accidental, unmanaged drift that happens gradually and unintentionally simply because different writers over time have quietly nudged the voice in slightly different, uncoordinated directions.\n\nWhen genuinely, deliberately evolving a brand voice — for instance, as a business matures from a scrappy, very casual startup register toward a more established, somewhat more polished and professional one as it grows and its customer base broadens — document the specific change clearly and explain the underlying reasoning behind it to everyone currently involved in writing content, so the shift happens consistently and coherently across all content and all writers, rather than inconsistently and unevenly, creating a genuinely confusing, disjointed patchwork of old-voice and new-voice content existing awkwardly side by side.\n\nEven amid genuine, deliberate voice evolution, some small core elements often deserve to remain genuinely constant as meaningful continuity anchors — perhaps a specific, well-established recurring phrase, a consistent way of greeting or signing off communications, or a specific enduring value the brand always consistently emphasizes regardless of how its broader overall tone shifts and matures over time — giving long-term, loyal audiences something genuinely familiar and recognizable to hold onto even as other, more surface-level aspects of the voice do continue to evolve and mature.",
